1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,640
Monday, a quarter past eight in the morning.

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:07,920
A buyer pays for a box of peonies, and at that exact moment, the bank's app restarts.

3
00:00:07,970 --> 00:00:12,192
Malika ships only when the bank tells her shop that a payment is paid.

4
00:00:12,242 --> 00:00:16,464
So the question is simple: will that news ever arrive?

5
00:00:16,514 --> 00:00:27,480
In Lesson 27, a payment ended paid or declined, in Lesson 28, even a silent one found its end, and in Lesson 1, the shop's server was told how its payment ended.

6
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:32,088
This lesson makes sure that news is never lost, and never invented.

7
00:00:32,138 --> 00:00:34,368
The naive way takes two steps.

8
00:00:34,418 --> 00:00:37,944
The app saves the payment as paid, and then tells the shop.

9
00:00:37,994 --> 00:00:41,448
If it crashes between those steps, the shop never hears.

10
00:00:41,498 --> 00:00:45,096
Malika ships nothing, and the buyer has paid for nothing.

11
00:00:45,146 --> 00:00:47,496
Swap the order, and it is worse.

12
00:00:47,546 --> 00:00:51,120
The app tells the shop paid, then crashes before saving.

13
00:00:51,170 --> 00:00:55,296
Now Malika ships peonies for a payment that does not exist.

14
00:00:55,346 --> 00:00:58,608
Two separate steps can always be split by a crash.

15
00:00:58,658 --> 00:01:04,560
Most systems are built this way, and they work, until the one restart that lands in between.

16
00:01:04,610 --> 00:01:10,560
So when a payment reaches its end, such as paid or declined, its news is written to the outbox.

17
00:01:10,610 --> 00:01:14,208
The outbox is a tray inside the bank's own records.

18
00:01:14,258 --> 00:01:18,648
The news and the payment's final state are saved together, in one step.

19
00:01:18,698 --> 00:01:21,600
Either both are saved, or neither is.

20
00:01:21,650 --> 00:01:26,520
There is never a paid payment without its news, and never news without a paid payment.

21
00:01:26,570 --> 00:01:28,392
Next comes the relay.

22
00:01:28,442 --> 00:01:34,176
It keeps collecting the oldest unsent news from the outbox, and passes it to the bank's message stream.

23
00:01:34,226 --> 00:01:36,888
Several copies of the app may run at once.

24
00:01:36,938 --> 00:01:40,584
Each copy takes different news, so no two pick the same one.

25
00:01:40,634 --> 00:01:43,656
Every piece of news is labelled with its payment.

26
00:01:43,706 --> 00:01:48,408
So all the news about one payment travels in one line, always in order.

27
00:01:48,458 --> 00:01:50,616
Paid can never overtake created.

28
00:01:50,666 --> 00:01:55,416
The relay marks news as sent only after the stream confirms it has it.

29
00:01:55,466 --> 00:02:00,408
So consider a crash at the worst moment: after sending, but before marking.

30
00:02:00,458 --> 00:02:03,672
When the app starts again, it sends that news once more.

31
00:02:03,722 --> 00:02:05,568
That is at least once delivery.

32
00:02:05,618 --> 00:02:08,856
News may arrive twice, but never zero times.

33
00:02:08,906 --> 00:02:13,296
To make repeats harmless, every piece of news carries its own id.

34
00:02:13,346 --> 00:02:17,232
A reader who sees an id twice simply drops the second one.

35
00:02:17,282 --> 00:02:19,656
One more rule keeps this simple.

36
00:02:19,706 --> 00:02:21,912
The bank's records stay the truth.

37
00:02:21,962 --> 00:02:24,408
The stream only carries the news about it.

38
00:02:24,458 --> 00:02:26,688
Here is how it looks on an ordinary day.

39
00:02:26,738 --> 00:02:28,560
A buyer pays.

40
00:02:28,610 --> 00:02:31,200
In the merchant portal, the payment shows paid.

41
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:38,064
Malika opens it, and the webhooks section soon shows its first piece of news, on its way to her shop's server.

42
00:02:38,114 --> 00:02:39,960
Now back to Monday's restart.

43
00:02:40,010 --> 00:02:43,104
The bank's app stopped at the exact moment the peonies were paid.

44
00:02:43,154 --> 00:02:45,048
It started again a minute later.

45
00:02:45,098 --> 00:02:48,456
The payment is still paid, and its news still goes out.

46
00:02:48,506 --> 00:02:50,328
Nothing is missing.

47
00:02:50,378 --> 00:02:54,288
On Bekzod's server, that news may now arrive twice.

48
00:02:54,338 --> 00:02:56,280
Both copies carry the same id.

49
00:02:56,330 --> 00:02:59,280
His server keeps the first and drops the second.

50
00:02:59,330 --> 00:03:03,432
So Malika never waits forever for news lost in a crash.

51
00:03:03,482 --> 00:03:07,464
She never ships because of news about a payment that never happened.

52
00:03:07,514 --> 00:03:09,792
And she never sees refunded before paid.

53
00:03:09,842 --> 00:03:14,112
The bank never has to explain news that its own records do not show.

54
00:03:14,162 --> 00:03:17,304
Next: delivering the news to the merchant's server.
